Create one high-fidelity visual concept for the Urkaaaz Android Dashboard
screen. This is a screen-design study only; do not edit production code or
generate a production background or runtime asset.

## Reference images

- Attach the existing Loadout screen screenshot and the existing Campaign
  screen screenshot as the only visual references.
- Treat both images as equal references for Urkaaaz's visual language. Identify
  and reuse their shared design system: painterly fantasy scenery, dark
  translucent panels, carved wood and iron details, restrained warm-gold trim,
  pale readable text, and consistent fantasy-style buttons and frames.
- Do not copy either screen's complete layout or move its screen-specific
  controls onto the Dashboard. The Dashboard must feel like the same game, not
  a third visual theme.
- Use the screenshots for appearance only. Do not copy campaign marker states,
  loadout selections, counts, or other temporary values as permanent game
  data.

## Output

- Generate exactly one PNG: `dashboard_screen_concept_01.png`.
- Save it under `assets_src/dashboard/screen-concepts/`.
- Canvas: exactly 1920 × 1080 pixels, landscape 16:9.
- Show the complete Android screen edge-to-edge. No phone frame, device mockup,
  presentation board, annotations, alternate screens, watermark, or signature.
- This is a visual concept for review. Keep background, panels, controls,
  icons, and text visually separable so the screen can be rebuilt with native
  Android views and individual assets. Do not flatten the interface into one
  production background.

## Dashboard content and hierarchy

- Design a welcoming, uncluttered game hub with one unmistakable primary
  action: `CAMPAIGN`. This action opens the Campaign screen; selecting a
  campaign level leads to Loadout before a match starts.
- Include a secondary `SHOP` navigation control, but do not show purchasable
  items, prices, or a functioning store. The Shop screen is currently a
  placeholder.
- Include a `BACK` control to return to the preceding start screen.
- Show a compact player summary using a framed avatar/portrait treatment that
  matches the reference screens. Do not add a login, online profile, avatar
  picker, or social features.
- Show the existing profile summary as live UI: `LEVEL 1`, `5` lives, `0`
  gold, and `N/A` for life regeneration. These values represent the current
  fresh-profile example only; do not bake them into artwork or imply that
  lives regenerate.
- Include one small, visually subordinate `RESET` utility control. It must not
  resemble a primary action; in the real app this action requires confirmation
  before clearing local player progress.
- Do not add a direct `LOADOUT` or `START MATCH` route from Dashboard. The
  current campaign flow proceeds through Campaign level selection and then
  Loadout.
- Keep all text concise, correctly spelled, and in English, consistent with
  the supplied screens. Use live UI text rather than lettering baked into
  decorative art.

## Language control

- Include a clearly recognizable language-change icon button in the top
  header, positioned at the upper-right and visually aligned with the other
  header controls.
- Use a neutral globe or translation-language symbol with the same warm-gold
  trim and dark button treatment used elsewhere. Make it read as a separate
  tappable control, not a decorative emblem.
- Use no national flag, invented language name, language dropdown, or extra
  settings panel; this concept requires the icon only.

## Layout direction

- Use a full-bleed, painterly Urkaaaz fantasy landscape in the same visual
  atmosphere as the attached screens. Keep the central area readable and
  provide enough contrast behind every control.
- Place a compact, wide top header containing the game/dashboard title, the
  framed player portrait, and the live level/lives/gold summary. Keep the
  language icon visible at the far right.
- Give the Campaign action the strongest visual emphasis in the central area,
  supported by a restrained campaign-themed illustration or preview that does
  not imitate the Campaign screen's map, route, markers, or chapter controls.
- Place `SHOP` and `BACK` as clearly secondary actions with consistent sizing,
  spacing, and button styling. Keep the small `RESET` utility separated from
  the primary navigation.
- Balance the composition for a landscape mobile screen: generous safe
  margins, large touch-friendly controls, clear visual hierarchy, and no
  overlap between text, icons, and artwork.

## Shared component treatment

- Reuse the same palette and material cues visible in both references:
  painterly scenery; charcoal or dark iron surfaces; carved wood accents;
  restrained bronze/gold edging; and high-contrast pale text.
- Match their header/frame proportions, corner treatments, border thickness,
  button depth, selected/highlight gold, and level of ornament. Avoid making
  every surface equally ornate; reserve the brightest gold emphasis for the
  Campaign action.
- Use the game's Cinzel SemiBold-style typography for prominent headings and
  button labels, with clear, compact supporting text.
- Keep avatar art, icons, labels, counters, backgrounds, and panel surfaces as
  distinct visual layers. Do not bake readable UI text into the scenery.

## Negative requirements

- No different visual theme from the attached Loadout and Campaign screens.
- No copied Loadout carousels, spell/unit/ammunition cards, or `START MATCH`
  button.
- No copied Campaign map, chapter selector, level markers, route, or
  `START LEVEL` button.
- No new gameplay mode, direct match launch, direct Loadout navigation,
  achievements, daily rewards, quests, tutorial, online features, or social
  features.
- No shop catalog, item prices, purchase controls, fake gold rewards, or
  fabricated profile values beyond the specified example.
- No fake life timer or claim that lives regenerate.
- No advertising, reward-video, or social-share buttons.
- No flags, text-heavy language selector, or invented localization options.
- No cluttered HUD, unrelated battle UI, decorative pseudo-text, gibberish,
  stretched scenery, device bezel, watermark, or signature.

## Review checklist

- Confirm the result is one complete 1920 × 1080 landscape Dashboard concept
  with no device frame.
- Confirm its visual language clearly combines the shared style of the
  attached Loadout and Campaign references without copying either screen's
  layout.
- Confirm `CAMPAIGN` is the primary action and `SHOP`, `BACK`, and `RESET`
  remain secondary and visually distinct.
- Confirm the profile summary shows the specified example values as live UI,
  not as part of the background artwork.
- Confirm the language-change icon is clearly visible, tappable-looking,
  neutral rather than flag-based, and aligned with the header.
- Confirm the composition does not imply a direct Loadout/match route,
  working shop, life regeneration, or other unimplemented feature.
- Use the concept for visual review only; do not import it as a single
  runtime screen background.
